Stride & Base =C2=B7 Weekly for patient runners

HOW TO TAPER WITHOUT LOSING YOUR MIND

The taper is when all the hard work turns into speed, and it's when runners f=
eel worst. Legs feel heavy, every twinge seems serious, and the urge to "test=
 fitness" is strong. Some guidelines:

- Length: 10 to 14 days for a half marathon, 2 to 3 weeks for a marathon.
- Volume: cut it by 30 to 50 percent, gradually. Keep the frequency, so run a=
s many days, just shorter.
- Intensity: keep it. A short session at race pace or faster 4 to 5 days befo=
re the race keeps the legs sharp. Example: 6 x 800m at 10K pace with full rec=
overy.
- Sleep and food: more of the first, normal amounts of the second. Many runne=
rs undereat in taper week and feel flat.
- Nothing new: shoes, gels, breakfast, socks. Everything on race day should b=
e something you've done before.

The flat, heavy feeling in the first week of taper is common and usually gone=
 by the final days. Trust the block.

RACE-DAY PACING
For a half, aim to run the first 5K 5 to 10 seconds per mile slower than goal=
 pace, settle in through 15K, and then race the last 10 minutes. Negative spl=
its are rarely an accident.

READER RESULTS
Send us your fall race result and one thing you'd change about your build. We=
 publish the best ones in November.

